 7. ⛪ Youth sports take over Sunday 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 For generations, churches helped organize American family life around a shared weekly ritual. Now, for many families, youth sports are increasingly filling that role, Axios' Russell Contreras reports. 

 Why it matters: America is undergoing a rapid religious shift , with more people leaving religion and churches closing . Youth sports are one of the less visible drivers of the change. "The idea of missing church for sports is just assumed," Linda Flanagan, author of " Take Back the Game : How Money and Mania Are Ruining Kids' Sports — and Why It Matters," tells Axios. "It's just not even a consideration anymore."

 Among parents of children ages 5-17, Sunday religious participation fell from 26.8% in 2003-07 to 16.7% in 2021-24, per American Time Use Survey data. Youth sports participation jumped from 53.8% in 2022 to 58% in 2024, the third consecutive annual increase, according to the Aspen Institute's Project Play . Read on . 

 
 
